    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAPI assertions verify expected properties of an HTTP response, such as status, headers, schema, and important field values.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define API assertions accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: API assertions verify expected properties of an HTTP response, such as status, headers, schema, and important field values.\n2. Use a concrete case: For `POST /orders`, assert the expected success status, order identifier, state, and error behavior for invalid input.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Overly brittle assertions can fail on harmless response changes; assert the contract that clients actually depend on.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define API assertions first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `POST /orders`, assert the expected success status, order identifier, state, and error behavior for invalid input.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use API assertions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for `POST /orders`, assert the expected success status, order identifier, state, and error behavior for invalid input. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor API assertions, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
