    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 1-2 Years of expirance interview question about API assertions.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen API assertions misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to API assertions. API assertions verify expected properties of an HTTP response, such as status, headers, schema, and important field values.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls API assertions: API assertions verify expected properties of an HTTP response, such as status, headers, schema, and important field values.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: For `POST /orders`, assert the expected success status, order identifier, state, and error behavior for invalid input.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Overly brittle assertions can fail on harmless response changes; assert the contract that clients actually depend on.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nFor `POST /orders`, assert the expected success status, order identifier, state, and error behavior for invalid input.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use API assertions when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for `POST /orders`, assert the expected success status, order identifier, state, and error behavior for invalid input. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
