    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDatabase testing verifies data correctness, persistence, constraints, queries, and the effect of application operations on stored state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Database testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Database testing verifies data correctness, persistence, constraints, queries, and the effect of application operations on stored state.\n2. Use a concrete case: Create an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Tests should avoid depending on implementation details that can change without affecting the data contract.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Database testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nCreate an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Database testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, create an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Database testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
