    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Database testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Database testing. Database testing verifies data correctness, persistence, constraints, queries, and the effect of application operations on stored state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Database testing: Database testing verifies data correctness, persistence, constraints, queries, and the effect of application operations on stored state.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Create an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Tests should avoid depending on implementation details that can change without affecting the data contract.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nCreate an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Database testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, create an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Database testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Database testing. Database testing verifies data correctness, persistence, constraints, queries, and the effect of application operations on stored state.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Database testing: Database testing verifies data correctness, persistence, constraints, queries, and the effect of application operations on stored state.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Create an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Tests should avoid depending on implementation details that can change without affecting the data contract.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nCreate an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Database testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, create an order through the API, then verify the corresponding rows, foreign-key relationships, and transaction outcome in the test database. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
