    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nSelenium automates browsers through WebDriver and can verify user-visible web behavior across supported browsers.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Selenium accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Selenium automates browsers through WebDriver and can verify user-visible web behavior across supported browsers.\n2. Use a concrete case: A Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Browser automation is valuable for critical journeys but slower and more brittle than lower-level API or unit tests.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Selenium first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Selenium when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Selenium as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Selenium.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Selenium, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
