    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Selenium when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Selenium.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Selenium automates browsers through WebDriver and can verify user-visible web behavior across supported browsers.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Browser automation is valuable for critical journeys but slower and more brittle than lower-level API or unit tests.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Selenium when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Selenium as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Selenium.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Selenium when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Selenium.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Selenium automates browsers through WebDriver and can verify user-visible web behavior across supported browsers.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: A Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Browser automation is valuable for critical journeys but slower and more brittle than lower-level API or unit tests.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nA Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Selenium when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a Selenium test can log into a test account, navigate to orders, and verify that a created order appears in the UI. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Selenium as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Selenium.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
