    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Locators is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. A locator identifies a UI element so automation can interact with it. Prefer stable attributes or accessible selectors over brittle position- or style-based selectors.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: A locator identifies a UI element so automation can interact with it. Prefer stable attributes or accessible selectors over brittle position- or style-based selectors.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: A button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Stable locators reduce maintenance, but the best selector depends on the framework and the application’s accessibility semantics.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nA button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Locators when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Locators as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Locators.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
