    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Locators are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: A locator identifies a UI element so automation can interact with it. Prefer stable attributes or accessible selectors over brittle position- or style-based selectors.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Locators as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Locators.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: A button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nA button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Locators when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Locators as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Locators.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Locators. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
