    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Locators misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Locators. A locator identifies a UI element so automation can interact with it. Prefer stable attributes or accessible selectors over brittle position- or style-based selectors.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Locators: A locator identifies a UI element so automation can interact with it. Prefer stable attributes or accessible selectors over brittle position- or style-based selectors.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Stable locators reduce maintenance, but the best selector depends on the framework and the application’s accessibility semantics.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Locators when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Locators as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Locators.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
