    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLocators is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Stable locators reduce maintenance, but the best selector depends on the framework and the application’s accessibility semantics. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Locators gives you: A locator identifies a UI element so automation can interact with it. Prefer stable attributes or accessible selectors over brittle position- or style-based selectors.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Stable locators reduce maintenance, but the best selector depends on the framework and the application’s accessibility semantics.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Locators when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a button with `data-testid=\"submit-order\"` or a stable accessible role/name is usually more resilient than an XPath tied to nested `<div>` structure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Locators as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Locators.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
