    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUI automation waits synchronize test actions with asynchronous application state. Explicit or condition-based waits are generally safer than arbitrary sleeps.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Waits accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: UI automation waits synchronize test actions with asynchronous application state. Explicit or condition-based waits are generally safer than arbitrary sleeps.\n2. Use a concrete case: Wait for the order status element to become visible or enabled instead of sleeping for two seconds and hoping the page is ready.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Poor waits cause flaky tests; waits should target observable conditions with sensible timeouts.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Waits first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nWait for the order status element to become visible or enabled instead of sleeping for two seconds and hoping the page is ready.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Waits when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, wait for the order status element to become visible or enabled instead of sleeping for two seconds and hoping the page is ready. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Waits as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Waits.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Waits, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
