    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Page objects misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Page objects. The Page Object pattern encapsulates UI locators and interactions so tests express business actions instead of repeating low-level selectors.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Page objects: The Page Object pattern encapsulates UI locators and interactions so tests express business actions instead of repeating low-level selectors.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A `CheckoutPage` can expose `enterAddress()` and `placeOrder()` while keeping the actual selectors inside the page object.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Too much abstraction can hide important test intent, so page objects should stay focused on reusable UI behavior.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA `CheckoutPage` can expose `enterAddress()` and `placeOrder()` while keeping the actual selectors inside the page object.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Page objects when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a `CheckoutPage` can expose `enterAddress()` and `placeOrder()` while keeping the actual selectors inside the page object. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Page objects as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Page objects.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
