    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCI testing runs automated checks as part of change integration so defects are detected before code reaches later environments.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define CI testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CI testing runs automated checks as part of change integration so defects are detected before code reaches later environments.\n2. Use a concrete case: A pull request can run unit and API tests first, then a smaller browser suite before merge, with failures blocking the protected branch.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Fast tests should run early; slow or environment-heavy checks may need separate stages.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define CI testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA pull request can run unit and API tests first, then a smaller browser suite before merge, with failures blocking the protected branch.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use CI testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a pull request can run unit and API tests first, then a smaller browser suite before merge, with failures blocking the protected branch. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor CI testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
