    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because cI testing runs automated checks as part of change integration so defects are detected before code reaches later environments. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a pull request can run unit and API tests first, then a smaller browser suite before merge, with failures blocking the protected branch.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how CI testing addresses it: CI testing runs automated checks as part of change integration so defects are detected before code reaches later environments.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A pull request can run unit and API tests first, then a smaller browser suite before merge, with failures blocking the protected branch.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Fast tests should run early; slow or environment-heavy checks may need separate stages.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA pull request can run unit and API tests first, then a smaller browser suite before merge, with failures blocking the protected branch.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use CI testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a pull request can run unit and API tests first, then a smaller browser suite before merge, with failures blocking the protected branch. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
