    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nFlaky tests is a concept used in QA 1-2 Years of expirance to address a defined engineering or business need.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Flaky tests accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Flaky tests is a concept used in QA 1-2 Years of expirance to address a defined engineering or business need.\n2. Use a concrete case: Track a test that passes and fails without a product change, reproduce it independently, and remove timing, data-order, or shared-environment dependencies before trusting the signal.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Flaky tests first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nTrack a test that passes and fails without a product change, reproduce it independently, and remove timing, data-order, or shared-environment dependencies before trusting the signal.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Flaky tests when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, track a test that passes and fails without a product change, reproduce it independently, and remove timing, data-order, or shared-environment dependencies before trusting the signal. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Flaky tests, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
