    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Flaky tests misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Flaky tests. Flaky tests is a concept used in QA 1-2 Years of expirance to address a defined engineering or business need.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Flaky tests: Flaky tests is a concept used in QA 1-2 Years of expirance to address a defined engineering or business need.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Track a test that passes and fails without a product change, reproduce it independently, and remove timing, data-order, or shared-environment dependencies before trusting the signal.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Broader coverage can improve confidence, but execution time, flakiness, and maintenance cost grow if checks are added without regard to risk.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nTrack a test that passes and fails without a product change, reproduce it independently, and remove timing, data-order, or shared-environment dependencies before trusting the signal.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn application test workflow may use Flaky tests when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, track a test that passes and fails without a product change, reproduce it independently, and remove timing, data-order, or shared-environment dependencies before trusting the signal. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
