    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 2-4 Years of interview question about Automation strategy.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAn automation strategy defines which tests to automate, at what level, with which tools, and how the suite will be maintained and measured.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Automation strategy accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: An automation strategy defines which tests to automate, at what level, with which tools, and how the suite will be maintained and measured.\n2. Use a concrete case: Automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Automation has maintenance cost, so automate checks that provide repeatable value rather than chasing a target percentage.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Automation strategy first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAutomate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Automation strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Automation strategy, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
