    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because an automation strategy defines which tests to automate, at what level, with which tools, and how the suite will be maintained and measured. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Automation strategy addresses it: An automation strategy defines which tests to automate, at what level, with which tools, and how the suite will be maintained and measured.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Automation has maintenance cost, so automate checks that provide repeatable value rather than chasing a target percentage.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nAutomate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Automation strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
