    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of Automation strategy comes from the behavior it provides: An automation strategy defines which tests to automate, at what level, with which tools, and how the suite will be maintained and measured. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: An automation strategy defines which tests to automate, at what level, with which tools, and how the suite will be maintained and measured.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n3. State the trade-off: Automation has maintenance cost, so automate checks that provide repeatable value rather than chasing a target percentage.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nAutomate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Automation strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
