    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 2-4 Years of interview question about Automation strategy.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAutomation strategy is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Automation has maintenance cost, so automate checks that provide repeatable value rather than chasing a target percentage. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Automation strategy gives you: An automation strategy defines which tests to automate, at what level, with which tools, and how the suite will be maintained and measured.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Automation has maintenance cost, so automate checks that provide repeatable value rather than chasing a target percentage.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: Automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nAutomate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Automation strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, automate stable, repeatable, high-value checks first; keep exploratory and highly visual checks where automation would be brittle or expensive. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
