    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAutomation selection chooses scenarios based on repeatability, business value, stability, execution cost, and risk.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Automation selection accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Automation selection chooses scenarios based on repeatability, business value, stability, execution cost, and risk.\n2. Use a concrete case: A deterministic tax calculation is a strong automation candidate; a frequently changing visual prototype may not be.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A test can be technically automatable yet economically poor to automate.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Automation selection first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA deterministic tax calculation is a strong automation candidate; a frequently changing visual prototype may not be.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Automation selection when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a deterministic tax calculation is a strong automation candidate; a frequently changing visual prototype may not be. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Automation selection, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
