    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because automation selection chooses scenarios based on repeatability, business value, stability, execution cost, and risk. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: a deterministic tax calculation is a strong automation candidate; a frequently changing visual prototype may not be.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Automation selection addresses it: Automation selection chooses scenarios based on repeatability, business value, stability, execution cost, and risk.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: A deterministic tax calculation is a strong automation candidate; a frequently changing visual prototype may not be.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: A test can be technically automatable yet economically poor to automate.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nA deterministic tax calculation is a strong automation candidate; a frequently changing visual prototype may not be.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Automation selection when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a deterministic tax calculation is a strong automation candidate; a frequently changing visual prototype may not be. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
