    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAutomation ROI compares the value of faster or repeatable feedback against creation, infrastructure, and maintenance cost.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define ROI accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Automation ROI compares the value of faster or repeatable feedback against creation, infrastructure, and maintenance cost.\n2. Use a concrete case: If a regression suite runs every day and catches expensive defects, automating its stable checks may quickly pay back compared with manual repetition.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: ROI should use actual execution and maintenance costs rather than optimistic assumptions.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define ROI first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a regression suite runs every day and catches expensive defects, automating its stable checks may quickly pay back compared with manual repetition.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use ROI when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a regression suite runs every day and catches expensive defects, automating its stable checks may quickly pay back compared with manual repetition. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating ROI as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with ROI.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor ROI, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
