    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Selenium Playwright are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Selenium and Playwright both automate browsers, but their APIs, browser-management models, parallelism, and features differ. The right choice depends on the application, team skills, and ecosystem.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Treating Selenium Playwright as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Selenium Playwright.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Evaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nEvaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Selenium Playwright when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, evaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Selenium Playwright as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Selenium Playwright.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Selenium Playwright. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
