    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 2-4 Years of interview question about Selenium Playwright.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Selenium Playwright misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Selenium Playwright. Selenium and Playwright both automate browsers, but their APIs, browser-management models, parallelism, and features differ. The right choice depends on the application, team skills, and ecosystem.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Selenium Playwright: Selenium and Playwright both automate browsers, but their APIs, browser-management models, parallelism, and features differ. The right choice depends on the application, team skills, and ecosystem.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Evaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Tool choice is not a quality strategy by itself; maintainability and test design matter more than brand preference.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nEvaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Selenium Playwright when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, evaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Selenium Playwright as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Selenium Playwright.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
