    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 2-4 Years of interview question about Selenium Playwright.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Selenium Playwright when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Selenium Playwright.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Selenium and Playwright both automate browsers, but their APIs, browser-management models, parallelism, and features differ. The right choice depends on the application, team skills, and ecosystem.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Evaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Tool choice is not a quality strategy by itself; maintainability and test design matter more than brand preference.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nEvaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Selenium Playwright when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, evaluate both against browser coverage, test isolation, debugging, CI runtime, and existing framework support before standardizing. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Selenium Playwright as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Selenium Playwright.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
