    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nAPI automation verifies service contracts, business rules, status codes, schemas, and important integration behavior without requiring a browser.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define API automation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: API automation verifies service contracts, business rules, status codes, schemas, and important integration behavior without requiring a browser.\n2. Use a concrete case: An API suite can create an order, assert the response contract, verify persistence, and clean up its data without depending on browser rendering.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: API tests are fast, but they cannot replace checks that depend on real browser behavior or visual interaction.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define API automation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API suite can create an order, assert the response contract, verify persistence, and clean up its data without depending on browser rendering.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use API automation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API suite can create an order, assert the response contract, verify persistence, and clean up its data without depending on browser rendering. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor API automation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
