    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nContract testing verifies that a provider and consumer agree on the API contract they rely on, reducing integration surprises.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Contract testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Contract testing verifies that a provider and consumer agree on the API contract they rely on, reducing integration surprises.\n2. Use a concrete case: A consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Contracts should describe real dependencies; excessive coupling to incidental fields reduces service independence.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Contract testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Contract testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Contract testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
