    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Contract testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Contract testing. Contract testing verifies that a provider and consumer agree on the API contract they rely on, reducing integration surprises.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Contract testing: Contract testing verifies that a provider and consumer agree on the API contract they rely on, reducing integration surprises.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: A consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Contracts should describe real dependencies; excessive coupling to incidental fields reduces service independence.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nA consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Contract testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
