    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nContract testing is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Contracts should describe real dependencies; excessive coupling to incidental fields reduces service independence. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Contract testing gives you: Contract testing verifies that a provider and consumer agree on the API contract they rely on, reducing integration surprises.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Contracts should describe real dependencies; excessive coupling to incidental fields reduces service independence.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: A consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nA consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Contract testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a consumer-provider contract can verify that an API change preserves the fields and behavior clients depend on without running every end-to-end workflow. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
