    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 2-4 Years of interview question about Integration testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Integration testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Integration testing. Integration tests verify that components work together across a meaningful boundary, such as application-to-database or service-to-service communication.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Integration testing: Integration tests verify that components work together across a meaningful boundary, such as application-to-database or service-to-service communication.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: An API integration test can start the service with a test database, call the endpoint, and verify the persisted result and returned status together.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Integration tests give stronger evidence than isolated unit tests but cost more to run and maintain.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAn API integration test can start the service with a test database, call the endpoint, and verify the persisted result and returned status together.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Integration testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, an API integration test can start the service with a test database, call the endpoint, and verify the persisted result and returned status together. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
