    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Database strategy misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Database strategy. A database test strategy determines which data behavior is validated at unit, integration, migration, and end-to-end levels.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Database strategy: A database test strategy determines which data behavior is validated at unit, integration, migration, and end-to-end levels.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use fast repository tests with realistic database integration tests for transaction, constraint, and query behavior that mocks cannot prove.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Trying to run the entire suite against production-like data on every change can be expensive and risky.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse fast repository tests with realistic database integration tests for transaction, constraint, and query behavior that mocks cannot prove.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Database strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use fast repository tests with realistic database integration tests for transaction, constraint, and query behavior that mocks cannot prove. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Database strategy as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Database strategy.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
