    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 2-4 Years of interview question about Performance planning.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPerformance planning defines target workloads, latency or throughput goals, environments, data volume, and how results will be interpreted before testing begins.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Performance planning accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Performance planning defines target workloads, latency or throughput goals, environments, data volume, and how results will be interpreted before testing begins.\n2. Use a concrete case: For an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Synthetic workloads are only useful when they represent important real usage patterns.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Performance planning first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nFor an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Performance planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Performance planning, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
