    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because performance planning defines target workloads, latency or throughput goals, environments, data volume, and how results will be interpreted before testing begins. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: for an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Performance planning addresses it: Performance planning defines target workloads, latency or throughput goals, environments, data volume, and how results will be interpreted before testing begins.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: For an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Synthetic workloads are only useful when they represent important real usage patterns.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nFor an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Performance planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because performance planning defines target workloads, latency or throughput goals, environments, data volume, and how results will be interpreted before testing begins. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: for an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Performance planning addresses it: Performance planning defines target workloads, latency or throughput goals, environments, data volume, and how results will be interpreted before testing begins.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: For an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Synthetic workloads are only useful when they represent important real usage patterns.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nFor an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Performance planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
