    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 2-4 Years of interview question about Performance planning.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nPerformance planning is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Synthetic workloads are only useful when they represent important real usage patterns. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Performance planning gives you: Performance planning defines target workloads, latency or throughput goals, environments, data volume, and how results will be interpreted before testing begins.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Synthetic workloads are only useful when they represent important real usage patterns.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: For an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nFor an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Performance planning when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for an API expected to handle 500 requests per second, define the mix of reads/writes and acceptable p95 latency before the load test. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Relying on intuition instead of the actual query plan.\n- Ignoring row counts and data distribution.\n- Fixing one query while creating a write, memory, or locking problem elsewhere.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
