    shortDescription: "Frequently asked QA 2-4 Years of interview question about Load testing.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nLoad testing measures system behavior under an expected level of concurrent or request load.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Load testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Load testing measures system behavior under an expected level of concurrent or request load.\n2. Use a concrete case: Generate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Passing a load test in one environment does not guarantee production performance if capacity and dependencies differ.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Load testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nGenerate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Load testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, generate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Load testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
