    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Load testing is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Load testing measures system behavior under an expected level of concurrent or request load.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Load testing measures system behavior under an expected level of concurrent or request load.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Generate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Passing a load test in one environment does not guarantee production performance if capacity and dependencies differ.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nGenerate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Load testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, generate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Load testing is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Load testing measures system behavior under an expected level of concurrent or request load.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Load testing measures system behavior under an expected level of concurrent or request load.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Generate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Passing a load test in one environment does not guarantee production performance if capacity and dependencies differ.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nGenerate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Load testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, generate a realistic request mix at increasing concurrency and measure latency, throughput, errors, and resource saturation rather than looking only at average response time. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
