    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nStress testing pushes a system beyond expected load to learn where it degrades and how it recovers.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Stress testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Stress testing pushes a system beyond expected load to learn where it degrades and how it recovers.\n2. Use a concrete case: Push the system beyond expected capacity to observe where it fails, whether it degrades predictably, and whether it recovers cleanly afterwards.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: The goal is to learn failure characteristics, not to prove that unlimited load is supported.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Stress testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nPush the system beyond expected capacity to observe where it fails, whether it degrades predictably, and whether it recovers cleanly afterwards.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Stress testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, push the system beyond expected capacity to observe where it fails, whether it degrades predictably, and whether it recovers cleanly afterwards. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Stress testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
