    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Stress testing in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Push the system beyond expected capacity to observe where it fails, whether it degrades predictably, and whether it recovers cleanly afterwards.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Stress testing pushes a system beyond expected load to learn where it degrades and how it recovers.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Push the system beyond expected capacity to observe where it fails, whether it degrades predictably, and whether it recovers cleanly afterwards.\n4. Finish with the limitation: The goal is to learn failure characteristics, not to prove that unlimited load is supported.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nPush the system beyond expected capacity to observe where it fails, whether it degrades predictably, and whether it recovers cleanly afterwards.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Stress testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, push the system beyond expected capacity to observe where it fails, whether it degrades predictably, and whether it recovers cleanly afterwards. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
