    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with Soak testing are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: Soak testing runs a workload for an extended period to reveal leaks, resource exhaustion, queue growth, or long-term degradation.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: Run a representative workload for a long period to reveal memory growth, connection leaks, queue buildup, or other degradation that short tests miss.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nRun a representative workload for a long period to reveal memory growth, connection leaks, queue buildup, or other degradation that short tests miss.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Soak testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, run a representative workload for a long period to reveal memory growth, connection leaks, queue buildup, or other degradation that short tests miss. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to Soak testing. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
