    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Soak testing in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: Run a representative workload for a long period to reveal memory growth, connection leaks, queue buildup, or other degradation that short tests miss.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: Soak testing runs a workload for an extended period to reveal leaks, resource exhaustion, queue growth, or long-term degradation.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: Run a representative workload for a long period to reveal memory growth, connection leaks, queue buildup, or other degradation that short tests miss.\n4. Finish with the limitation: A soak test needs stable infrastructure and enough duration to expose slow failures.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nRun a representative workload for a long period to reveal memory growth, connection leaks, queue buildup, or other degradation that short tests miss.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Soak testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, run a representative workload for a long period to reveal memory growth, connection leaks, queue buildup, or other degradation that short tests miss. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
