    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nMobile testing evaluates behavior across device sizes, OS versions, network conditions, permissions, lifecycle events, and resource constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Mobile testing accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Mobile testing evaluates behavior across device sizes, OS versions, network conditions, permissions, lifecycle events, and resource constraints.\n2. Use a concrete case: Run the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: A device matrix should reflect real usage and risk rather than attempting every device permutation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Mobile testing first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nRun the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Mobile testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, run the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Mobile testing, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
