    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Mobile testing misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Mobile testing. Mobile testing evaluates behavior across device sizes, OS versions, network conditions, permissions, lifecycle events, and resource constraints.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Mobile testing: Mobile testing evaluates behavior across device sizes, OS versions, network conditions, permissions, lifecycle events, and resource constraints.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Run the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: A device matrix should reflect real usage and risk rather than attempting every device permutation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nRun the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Mobile testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, run the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
