    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you keep Mobile testing reliable as the product and test suite grow?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Mobile testing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Mobile testing evaluates behavior across device sizes, OS versions, network conditions, permissions, lifecycle events, and resource constraints.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Run the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: A device matrix should reflect real usage and risk rather than attempting every device permutation.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nRun the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Mobile testing when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, run the critical flow across supported OS versions, screen sizes, network states, and app lifecycle events rather than relying on one device. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
