    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Quality gates misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Quality gates. Quality gates are explicit conditions a change must satisfy before progressing, such as required tests, security checks, or defect thresholds.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Quality gates: Quality gates are explicit conditions a change must satisfy before progressing, such as required tests, security checks, or defect thresholds.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Require a small set of meaningful checks such as build health, critical regression results, security findings, and deployment readiness before release promotion.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Gates should be tied to meaningful risk; too many low-value gates slow delivery without improving confidence.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nRequire a small set of meaningful checks such as build health, critical regression results, security findings, and deployment readiness before release promotion.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nA quality-engineering program may use Quality gates when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, require a small set of meaningful checks such as build health, critical regression results, security findings, and deployment readiness before release promotion. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Quality gates as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Quality gates.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
