    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because a QA strategy defines how an organization will obtain sufficient evidence of product quality across the development lifecycle, balancing risk, speed, automation, environments, and operational feedback. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: for a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how QA strategy addresses it: A QA strategy defines how an organization will obtain sufficient evidence of product quality across the development lifecycle, balancing risk, speed, automation, environments, and operational feedback.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: For a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Strategy must evolve with architecture, customer impact, and failure history.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use QA strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
