    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe most common mistakes with QA strategy are usually misunderstandings of its actual behavior, poor handling of its constraints, or using it where a simpler approach would be enough. The important cases are below.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you have enough practical experience to recognize failure patterns rather than only describe the happy path.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Know the intended behavior: A QA strategy defines how an organization will obtain sufficient evidence of product quality across the development lifecycle, balancing risk, speed, automation, environments, and operational feedback.\n2. Watch for these common failure patterns:\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n3. Use the example to check whether the implementation really behaves as expected: For a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name two or three mistakes that are realistic for this concept and briefly explain why each one matters. I would finish with the one mistake I see most often in practice.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use QA strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nChoose mistakes that are specific to QA strategy. Avoid generic answers such as “not testing enough” unless you explain exactly what can go wrong.",
