    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQA strategy is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Strategy must evolve with architecture, customer impact, and failure history. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what QA strategy gives you: A QA strategy defines how an organization will obtain sufficient evidence of product quality across the development lifecycle, balancing risk, speed, automation, environments, and operational feedback.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Strategy must evolve with architecture, customer impact, and failure history.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: For a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use QA strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
