    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain QA strategy in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: For a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: A QA strategy defines how an organization will obtain sufficient evidence of product quality across the development lifecycle, balancing risk, speed, automation, environments, and operational feedback.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: For a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Strategy must evolve with architecture, customer impact, and failure history.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nFor a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use QA strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, for a multi-service platform, define risk tiers, test levels, critical journeys, automation boundaries, and production quality signals instead of relying on one regression suite. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
