    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQuality engineering treats quality as a shared engineering concern across design, development, testing, deployment, and operations rather than a final QA phase.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Quality engineering accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Quality engineering treats quality as a shared engineering concern across design, development, testing, deployment, and operations rather than a final QA phase.\n2. Use a concrete case: Developers, QA, and platform teams can improve testability, contract checks, observability, and safe deployment controls together rather than treating quality as a final handoff.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Broader ownership improves feedback but requires clear responsibilities and engineering discipline.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Quality engineering first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nDevelopers, QA, and platform teams can improve testability, contract checks, observability, and safe deployment controls together rather than treating quality as a final handoff.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Quality engineering when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, developers, QA, and platform teams can improve testability, contract checks, observability, and safe deployment controls together rather than treating quality as a final handoff. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Quality engineering as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Quality engineering.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Quality engineering, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
