    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nUI automation verifies real user interactions through a browser or device, typically for a focused set of critical flows.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define UI automation accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: UI automation verifies real user interactions through a browser or device, typically for a focused set of critical flows.\n2. Use a concrete case: Automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: UI tests provide strong system evidence but are slower and more sensitive to UI changes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define UI automation first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nAutomate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use UI automation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor UI automation, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
