    detailedAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe main value of UI automation comes from the behavior it provides: UI automation verifies real user interactions through a browser or device, typically for a focused set of critical flows. It can be useful when that behavior reduces a specific engineering or business problem, as in this example: Automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can distinguish a genuine benefit from a feature list and understand what the benefit costs.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Identify the behavior being provided: UI automation verifies real user interactions through a browser or device, typically for a focused set of critical flows.\n2. Connect it to the benefit shown by the example: Automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n3. State the trade-off: UI tests provide strong system evidence but are slower and more sensitive to UI changes.\n4. Avoid claiming a benefit that is not supported by the actual workload.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would name the practical benefits first, tie each one to the behavior, and then give the main trade-off. That shows I understand why the feature is valuable rather than simply listing advantages.\n\n**Example :**\nAutomate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use UI automation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain why the benefit matters for the workload. A benefit without a condition is usually too broad for a strong engineering answer.",
