    shortDescription: "Frequently asked Senior QA 5+ Years of expirance interview question about UI automation.",
    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen UI automation misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to UI automation. UI automation verifies real user interactions through a browser or device, typically for a focused set of critical flows.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls UI automation: UI automation verifies real user interactions through a browser or device, typically for a focused set of critical flows.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: UI tests provide strong system evidence but are slower and more sensitive to UI changes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nAutomate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use UI automation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
