    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “How would you diagnose UI automation when the issue appears only under slow network, heavy rendering, or large client-side work?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to UI automation.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: UI automation verifies real user interactions through a browser or device, typically for a focused set of critical flows.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: UI tests provide strong system evidence but are slower and more sensitive to UI changes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nAutomate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use UI automation when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, automate a small number of stable critical user journeys and keep selectors resilient to harmless UI refactoring. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Optimizing for test count instead of risk coverage.\n- Allowing flaky checks to become accepted background noise.\n- Making tests so tightly coupled to implementation that normal refactoring breaks them.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
