    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen API strategy misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to API strategy. An API test strategy defines contract, functional, negative, authorization, resilience, and integration coverage for service interfaces.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls API strategy: An API test strategy defines contract, functional, negative, authorization, resilience, and integration coverage for service interfaces.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Choose API checks around contracts, negative cases, authorization, data integrity, and critical workflows rather than maximizing endpoint count.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: API coverage should follow consumer behavior and business risk, not endpoint count alone.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nChoose API checks around contracts, negative cases, authorization, data integrity, and critical workflows rather than maximizing endpoint count.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use API strategy when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, choose API checks around contracts, negative cases, authorization, data integrity, and critical workflows rather than maximizing endpoint count. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Confusing authentication with authorization.\n- Using inconsistent status/error semantics.\n- Changing an API contract without considering existing clients.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
