    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because observability provides enough logs, metrics, traces, and contextual signals to understand system behavior and diagnose failures. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Observability addresses it: Observability provides enough logs, metrics, traces, and contextual signals to understand system behavior and diagnose failures.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: Use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Too much telemetry can increase cost and noise; signals should support specific operational questions.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nUse test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Observability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Observability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Observability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
