    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nThe usual flow for Observability is to start with the inputs or conditions, apply the behavior described here, and verify the resulting state. Observability provides enough logs, metrics, traces, and contextual signals to understand system behavior and diagnose failures.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis tests whether you understand the mechanism and can explain a sequence, not just the end result.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Establish the starting condition.\n2. Apply the mechanism: Observability provides enough logs, metrics, traces, and contextual signals to understand system behavior and diagnose failures.\n3. Verify the result using the concrete case: Use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n4. Watch the trade-off or failure condition: Too much telemetry can increase cost and noise; signals should support specific operational questions.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nWalk through the normal sequence in order. Explain what changes at each important step, then mention how you would verify the result and what you would inspect if the operation failed.\n\n**Example :**\nUse test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Observability when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use test failures together with logs, traces, and metrics to distinguish a product defect from a test-environment or infrastructure failure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Observability as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Observability.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nExplain the state change behind the steps. That is more useful than listing commands or buzzwords.",
