    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nShift left moves useful quality and defect-prevention activities earlier in the lifecycle, such as testable design, static checks, unit tests, and threat modeling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Shift left accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Shift left moves useful quality and defect-prevention activities earlier in the lifecycle, such as testable design, static checks, unit tests, and threat modeling.\n2. Use a concrete case: Catch requirement and design defects through reviews, static checks, unit tests, and contract tests before expensive end-to-end execution.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Shifting everything left is unrealistic; some behaviors only exist in integrated or production environments.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Shift left first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nCatch requirement and design defects through reviews, static checks, unit tests, and contract tests before expensive end-to-end execution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Shift left when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, catch requirement and design defects through reviews, static checks, unit tests, and contract tests before expensive end-to-end execution. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Shift left as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Shift left.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Shift left, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
