    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Shift left misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Shift left. Shift left moves useful quality and defect-prevention activities earlier in the lifecycle, such as testable design, static checks, unit tests, and threat modeling.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Shift left: Shift left moves useful quality and defect-prevention activities earlier in the lifecycle, such as testable design, static checks, unit tests, and threat modeling.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Catch requirement and design defects through reviews, static checks, unit tests, and contract tests before expensive end-to-end execution.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Shifting everything left is unrealistic; some behaviors only exist in integrated or production environments.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nCatch requirement and design defects through reviews, static checks, unit tests, and contract tests before expensive end-to-end execution.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Shift left when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, catch requirement and design defects through reviews, static checks, unit tests, and contract tests before expensive end-to-end execution. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Shift left as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Shift left.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
