    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Shift right misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Shift right. Shift right uses controlled production observation and experimentation to detect and manage risks after release, such as monitoring, feature flags, canaries, and rollback signals.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Shift right: Shift right uses controlled production observation and experimentation to detect and manage risks after release, such as monitoring, feature flags, canaries, and rollback signals.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: Use production monitoring, controlled rollout, synthetic checks, and incident feedback to learn about behavior after deployment.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Shift-right practices require safe deployment controls and strong observability to avoid turning users into unprotected test subjects.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nUse production monitoring, controlled rollout, synthetic checks, and incident feedback to learn about behavior after deployment.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Shift right when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, use production monitoring, controlled rollout, synthetic checks, and incident feedback to learn about behavior after deployment. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Shift right as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Shift right.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
