    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nCI gates are automated conditions that prevent a change from progressing when required evidence is missing or failing.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define CI gates accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: CI gates are automated conditions that prevent a change from progressing when required evidence is missing or failing.\n2. Use a concrete case: A pull request can be blocked when the build fails or a required test/security gate reports a high-confidence failure.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Gates should protect meaningful invariants; excessive gating can slow developers without improving outcomes.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define CI gates first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nA pull request can be blocked when the build fails or a required test/security gate reports a high-confidence failure.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use CI gates when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, a pull request can be blocked when the build fails or a required test/security gate reports a high-confidence failure. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating CI gates as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with CI gates.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor CI gates, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
