    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nRelease readiness is a risk-based assessment that the required functionality, quality evidence, operational controls, and known issues are acceptable for release.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Release readiness accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Release readiness is a risk-based assessment that the required functionality, quality evidence, operational controls, and known issues are acceptable for release.\n2. Use a concrete case: Review open high-risk defects, automation results, environment health, rollback capability, and known limitations before a release decision.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Readiness is a decision under uncertainty, not a claim that every possible defect has been eliminated.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Release readiness first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nReview open high-risk defects, automation results, environment health, rollback capability, and known limitations before a release decision.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Release readiness when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, review open high-risk defects, automation results, environment health, rollback capability, and known limitations before a release decision. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Release readiness as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Release readiness.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Release readiness, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
