    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nIt matters because a defect escape is a defect that reaches a later environment or production despite earlier quality controls. In practice, the example shows how that behavior is used: if a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThe question is looking for reasoning rather than another definition. Explain the problem the concept solves, then say what would make you choose a different approach.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State the problem or need.\n2. Explain how Defect escape addresses it: A defect escape is a defect that reaches a later environment or production despite earlier quality controls. Escape analysis looks for why existing controls did not detect it.\n3. Tie that benefit to the concrete situation: If a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n4. State the condition that can make the approach a poor fit: Counting escapes without learning their causes produces a metric rather than an improvement.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nAnswer the “why” directly, then connect it to a real engineering constraint. I would give one concrete situation and close by saying what would make me choose another approach.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Defect escape when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defect escape as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defect escape.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nDo not repeat the definition as your whole answer. Explain the problem, the practical value, and the condition that would change your decision.",
