    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nWhen Defect escape misbehaves, start by reproducing the symptom and verifying the assumptions around the feature. Then inspect the inputs, state, dependencies, and evidence most relevant to Defect escape. A defect escape is a defect that reaches a later environment or production despite earlier quality controls. Escape analysis looks for why existing controls did not detect it.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nTroubleshooting questions reveal whether you can narrow a problem using evidence instead of changing several things at once.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Reproduce the failure and capture the exact symptom.\n2. Check the part of the system that controls Defect escape: A defect escape is a defect that reaches a later environment or production despite earlier quality controls. Escape analysis looks for why existing controls did not detect it.\n3. Use the concrete workflow as a comparison point: If a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n4. Confirm the fix with a targeted regression check and watch for the trade-off: Counting escapes without learning their causes produces a metric rather than an improvement.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would reproduce the problem first and collect evidence before changing code. Then I would inspect the component or state most closely tied to the symptom, make one controlled change, and verify the result with a regression test or equivalent check.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Defect escape when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defect escape as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defect escape.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nTalk through your investigation order. Interviewers learn more from your evidence-gathering and narrowing strategy than from hearing a long list of possible causes.",
