    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nDefect escape is useful when its benefits justify the costs or complexity it introduces. Counting escapes without learning their causes produces a metric rather than an improvement. The decision should be based on the actual requirement, not on a blanket rule.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis question tests engineering judgment. The interviewer wants to hear both sides and the constraint that determines the choice.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. State what Defect escape gives you: A defect escape is a defect that reaches a later environment or production despite earlier quality controls. Escape analysis looks for why existing controls did not detect it.\n2. State what it costs or complicates: Counting escapes without learning their causes produces a metric rather than an improvement.\n3. Use this example to show where the trade-off appears: If a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n4. Say when you would choose a simpler alternative.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would explain the benefit first, then the cost, and finally the condition that makes the trade-off worthwhile. I would avoid presenting one side as universally correct.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Defect escape when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defect escape as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defect escape.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nMake the trade-off concrete. Say what improves, what gets harder, and which constraint makes the decision reasonable.",
