    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nI would explain Defect escape in plain language first, then give the technical detail that distinguishes it from nearby concepts. The practical anchor is: If a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can communicate the concept clearly at an interview pace and adjust the depth when the interviewer asks a follow-up.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Give a one-sentence definition: A defect escape is a defect that reaches a later environment or production despite earlier quality controls. Escape analysis looks for why existing controls did not detect it.\n2. Explain one important behavior or distinction.\n3. Use the example to make it concrete: If a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n4. Finish with the limitation: Counting escapes without learning their causes produces a metric rather than an improvement.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would keep the first answer concise, then wait for the interviewer to signal whether they want internals. If they go deeper, I would expand the mechanism and trade-offs rather than repeating the definition.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Defect escape when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defect escape as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defect escape.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nPractice saying {c} aloud in your own words. A technically correct answer is weaker if you cannot explain it without reading a script.",
