    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Defect escape when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Defect escape.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: A defect escape is a defect that reaches a later environment or production despite earlier quality controls. Escape analysis looks for why existing controls did not detect it.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: If a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Counting escapes without learning their causes produces a metric rather than an improvement.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nIf a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Defect escape when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, if a production defect escaped pre-release testing, trace the missed signal back to the requirement, test design, environment, automation, or process and add the missing control. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Defect escape as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Defect escape.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
