    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nQuality metrics provide evidence about defects, test effectiveness, reliability, and delivery risk, but should be interpreted in context.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can define Quality metrics accurately and explain what it changes in practice. The interviewer can then probe the boundary conditions, alternatives, or failure cases.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start with the definition: Quality metrics provide evidence about defects, test effectiveness, reliability, and delivery risk, but should be interpreted in context.\n2. Use a concrete case: Track measures that support decisions, such as escaped defects and flaky-test rate, rather than optimizing a vanity metric like raw test count.\n3. Keep the explanation tied to the behavior in the question.\n4. Mention the main limitation or trade-off: Metrics can drive unhealthy behavior if treated as targets without understanding their limitations.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would define Quality metrics first, then explain the behavior that matters in practice. I would use the concrete example to make the idea easy to visualize and finish with the main limitation.\n\n**Example :**\nTrack measures that support decisions, such as escaped defects and flaky-test rate, rather than optimizing a vanity metric like raw test count.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Quality metrics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, track measures that support decisions, such as escaped defects and flaky-test rate, rather than optimizing a vanity metric like raw test count. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Quality metrics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Quality metrics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nFor Quality metrics, give the definition first and then one consequence. That makes it clear you understand the concept instead of only recognizing the name.",
