    sampleAnswer: "**Direct answer :**\nA useful advanced follow-up is “What would you change about Quality metrics when the system grows or an important assumption stops being true?” The expected answer should connect the concept to scale, failure handling, compatibility, or another constraint relevant to Quality metrics.\n\n**Why this is asked :**\nThis checks whether you can take a basic concept and reason about what changes when the system becomes less ideal or more constrained.\n\n**Breaking it down :**\n1. Start from the normal behavior: Quality metrics provide evidence about defects, test effectiveness, reliability, and delivery risk, but should be interpreted in context.\n2. Introduce a harder condition such as higher load, failure, compatibility, or security.\n3. Use this concrete case as the anchor: Track measures that support decisions, such as escaped defects and flaky-test rate, rather than optimizing a vanity metric like raw test count.\n4. Explain how the original trade-off changes: Metrics can drive unhealthy behavior if treated as targets without understanding their limitations.\n\n**How to structure your spoken answer :**\nI would answer the follow-up by stating the changed constraint first. Then I would explain how I would adapt the design or implementation and what evidence I would use to validate the decision.\n\n**Example :**\nTrack measures that support decisions, such as escaped defects and flaky-test rate, rather than optimizing a vanity metric like raw test count.\n\n**Real-world use case :**\nAn organization-wide quality program may use Quality metrics when it needs the behavior described in the question. For example, track measures that support decisions, such as escaped defects and flaky-test rate, rather than optimizing a vanity metric like raw test count. The decision should still be checked against the workload, failure modes, and operational constraints.\n\n**Common mistakes :**\n- Treating Quality metrics as a definition to memorize rather than a behavior to apply.\n- Ignoring the main constraint or failure mode associated with Quality metrics.\n- Using it without checking whether the surrounding system actually needs it.\n\n**Interview tip :**\nA strong advanced answer changes with the constraint. Do not give the same basic definition again when the interviewer is testing senior-level reasoning.",
